// §A9 first slice (§14.7) — `@boot` + `@kernel_init`.
//
// The kernel's stage-1 entry chain has three distinct regimes:
//
//   @boot         pre-MMU code. Vestra in surface, assembly in
//                 runtime model. Naked function — no prologue,
//                 no epilogue, body is exactly the instructions
//                 the user writes. Tier V's hidden-pointer
//                 threading can't run here. Seven restrictions:
//                 no using row except Asm / RawMemory / Mmio,
//                 no call into ordinary Vestra, no throws,
//                 no spilled register arguments, no reference
//                 to non-@boot code except by &decl, comptime
//                 results must be Trivial, @naked implied.
//
//   @kernel_init  transitional regime. Ordinary Vestra surface
//                 with the full type system, single-threaded
//                 and pre-scheduler in runtime model. No
//                 using Async / spawn / await; allocator may
//                 or may not be live depending on call site;
//                 the only regime where @no_auto_barrier is
//                 admitted (§14.12.3). One-way: ordinary code
//                 may not call back into @kernel_init.
//
//   ordinary      everything after the scheduler's first
//                 swapContext. Full Vestra, multi-threaded,
//                 every cap available subject to discharge.
//
// A typical aarch64 stage-1 entry chain is
// `@boot func _start → @kernel_init func kernel_init →
//  ordinary func kernel_main`. The single br from _start to
// kernel_init is the audit-flagged boundary between boot
// and runtime; the call from kernel_init to kernel_main is
// the boundary between init regime and steady-state.

module examples.boot_chain

// Pre-MMU entry. In a real kernel this would be an asm block
// that sets up the initial stack pointer and `br &kernel_init`.
// On hosted v0.5 it stays empty — the [[gnu::naked]] attribute
// lands but there's no instruction sequence to execute (the
// example demonstrates the codegen shape, not boot semantics).
@boot
func _start() using Asm {
}

// Transitional regime. Brings up subsystems that have to run
// before the scheduler dispatches: page tables, vector table
// registration, allocator handoff, first task's context. The
// `using Alloc` row is admitted by sema; whether Alloc is
// actually live is an audit-time property of the call site.
@kernel_init
func kernel_init(_ marker: UInt64) using Alloc -> UInt64 {
    // The allocator has returned its @kernel_init initialiser
    // by this point — Alloc is live. Real kernels would mint a
    // Box[SchedulerState] here and tail-call swapContext into
    // it. The marker round-trip stands in for the "init ran"
    // observable signal we test from the C++ driver.
    return marker + 1
}

// Ordinary regime. Anything that runs once the scheduler has
// dispatched the first task. No @boot / @kernel_init markers.
func kernel_main(_ marker: UInt64) -> UInt64 {
    return marker + 100
}
